\description{
\code{gas()} constructs a generalized autoregressive score component (Creal et
al., 2013; Harvey, 2013): a level \eqn{f_t} that is added to the linear
predictor and driven by the score of the observation density at the
previous times,
\deqn{f_t = \omega + \sum_{i=1}^{p} \kappa_i s_{t-i} +
  \sum_{j=1}^{q} \xi_j f_{t-j},}
with \eqn{s_t = \partial \ell_t / \partial \eta_t} the derivative of the
log-likelihood contribution at time \eqn{t} with respect to the predictor
at which it is evaluated, multiplied by a power of the expected information
when \code{scaling} is not zero.
}
\details{
The term adds no columns. The predictor at one time depends on the
data at the previous ones, so the contribution cannot be written as a
block, and \code{\link[=term_filter]{term_filter()}} runs the recursion instead. For this reason the
term belongs to the structural branch.

The score of the distribution that the model carries drives the
recursion, so the same term is a GARCH-like volatility model when it
enters the scale of a Gaussian, a dynamic count model when it enters the
mean of a Poisson, and a robust location filter when it enters a Student
t. A heavy-tailed score is bounded in the observation, so an outlier
moves the level by a bounded amount instead of in proportion to its
size.

\subsection{The parameters and their chart}{
The parameters are the level \eqn{\omega}, the score loadings
\eqn{\kappa_1, \dots, \kappa_p}, and the persistence. Each is estimated on the
unconstrained scale of a link, and \code{links} overrides any of them;
the defaults are the following.

The level carries the identity link because it is unconstrained. The
loadings carry the log link: a positive loading responds in the direction
of the score, which is the case that the score-driven literature writes,
and positivity is then structural. A subformula develops the loading on the
log scale, so every group and every observation has a positive loading.
A loading that must be free in sign takes the identity link, which is set
with \code{links = list(kappa1 = linkfunctions7::identity_link())}.

The persistence is carried by partial autocorrelations and not by the
coefficients \eqn{\xi_j} directly. The stationary region of an
autoregression is not a box, so a collection of scalar links cannot
represent it, whereas the partial autocorrelations each range over
\eqn{(-1, 1)} independently and the Levinson-Durbin recursion maps them
bijectively onto the coefficients. At \eqn{q = 1} the two coincide. Each
coordinate is named after the chart on which it is estimated, \code{pacf1} and
so on, following the convention of \pkg{parameters7}.

With the default links, a parameter developed over groups or observations
stays in its own set. The development acts on the unconstrained scale, so
a loading on the log link is positive and a persistence on the rhobit link
is stationary at every observation.
}

\subsection{Several filtered parameters}{
A \code{gas()} term in the formula of each of several parameters, as in
\code{y ~ gas(p = 1, q = 1, time = t) | sigma ~ gas(p = 1, q = 1, time = t)},
gives one recursion for the vector of their levels. The score of each
filtered parameter is read at the levels of all the others at the same
time, so the levels cannot be filtered one after the other.
\code{\link[=gas_joint]{gas_joint()}} joins the terms, and a fitting layer reads them as one
structural term. The terms must walk the same series (the same \code{by} and
\code{time}).

The scaling is joint. With \eqn{G} the filtered parameters, the driving
vector is
\deqn{u_t = \mathcal{I}_{GG,t}^{-d}\,
  \frac{\partial \ell_t}{\partial \eta_{G,t}},}
where \eqn{\mathcal{I}_{GG,t}} is the expected information of the
filtered parameters with the other parameters held. Every term carries the
same \code{scaling}, and with more than one term it is 0, 1/2 or 1. For 1/2
the power is the symmetric square root, which does not depend on the order
of the parameters. Where the information is diagonal, as for the mean and
the scale of a gaussian, the joint scaling equals the scaling of each
parameter by its own information.

By default each level loads its own score only, so the matrices of
loadings are diagonal. The argument \code{p_cross} of a term names the other
filtered parameters whose scores its level loads as well, with the number
of lags of each: in
\code{y ~ gas(p = 1, q = 1, p_cross = c(sigma = 1)) | sigma ~ gas(p = 1, q = 1)}
the level of \code{mu} responds to the score of \code{sigma} at lag one, and the
level of \code{sigma} responds to its own score only, so the matrix of
loadings at lag one is upper triangular. A lag that \code{p_cross} leaves out
is a loading restricted to zero, and the number of lags of a partner may
exceed the term's own \code{p}. The parameter named in \code{p_cross} must carry a
\code{gas()} term of its own. A cross loading rides the identity link,
because its sign is free. For a gaussian, the loading of the score of the
mean in the level of the log-scale is the asymmetric response of the
volatility to the sign of a surprise, which is the term in
\eqn{z_{t-1}} of an EGARCH model. Each level keeps its own persistence,
so the autoregressive part is stationary when the part of each level is.
}

\subsection{The scaling of the score}{
The recursion is driven by the score multiplied by a power of the
expected information of the same predictor,
\deqn{s_t = \mathcal{I}_t^{-d}\,\frac{\partial \ell_t}{\partial \eta_t},
  \qquad \mathcal{I}_t = -\mathrm{E}\!\left[
  \frac{\partial^2 \ell_t}{\partial \eta_t^2}\right],}
where \eqn{d} is the argument \code{scaling} and \eqn{\mathcal{I}_t} is evaluated
at the predictor and at the other parameters of observation \eqn{t}. The
level is scalar, so \eqn{\mathcal{I}_t} is a number and every real
\eqn{d} defines a valid model. The literature uses three values
(Creal et al., 2013). With \eqn{d = 0}, the default, the score is used as
it is. With \eqn{d = 1/2} the scaled score has unit conditional variance.
With \eqn{d = 1} the update is the score premultiplied by the inverse
information, a step of Fisher scoring, and to first order it does not
depend on the link of the parameter. The value \eqn{d = 1} with the
identity link gives three known models exactly: GARCH(1, 1) for the
variance of a gaussian, the INGARCH(1, 1) model for the mean of a Poisson
and the ACD(1, 1) model for the mean of an exponential.

Wherever \eqn{\mathcal{I}_t} varies with the observation, the loadings
\eqn{\kappa_i} have different units for different values of \eqn{d}. Where it
is constant, as for the log-scale of a gaussian, changing \eqn{d} only
rescales the loadings and leaves the likelihood unchanged. A scaling
other than zero requires a univariate family whose expected information
has an analytic second derivative
(\code{\link[distributions7:distrib_d2expected_hessian]{distributions7::distrib_d2expected_hessian()}}), because the derivatives
of the filter reach the fourth derivative of \eqn{\mathcal{I}_t}, which is
read from the derivatives of the expected information up to
\code{\link[distributions7:distrib_d4expected_hessian]{distributions7::distrib_d4expected_hessian()}}.

\eqn{\mathcal{I}_t} is part of the model and is read in closed form at the
predictor of every step of the recursion. Every family of
\pkg{distributions7} carries the closed form. A family written with its
density alone supplies it through methods for
\code{\link[distributions7:distrib_expected_hessian]{distributions7::distrib_expected_hessian()}},
\code{\link[distributions7:distrib_dexpected_hessian]{distributions7::distrib_dexpected_hessian()}} and
\code{\link[distributions7:distrib_d2expected_hessian]{distributions7::distrib_d2expected_hessian()}}; without them only
\code{scaling = 0} is available. The two substitutes of \pkg{distributions7} for
a missing closed form do not apply here: the outer product of the scores
(\code{\link[distributions7:expect_opg]{distributions7::expect_opg()}}) reads \eqn{\mathcal{I}_t} at the data as
\eqn{(\partial \ell_t/\partial \eta_t)^2}, which gives
\eqn{s_t = (\partial \ell_t/\partial \eta_t)\,
|\partial \ell_t/\partial \eta_t|^{-2d}} and so defines another model, and a
quadrature
(\code{\link[distributions7:expect_integrate]{distributions7::expect_integrate()}}) would be evaluated once per step and
per derivative order, in sequence.
}

\subsection{The level beside an intercept}{
The level \eqn{\omega} adds a constant to the recursion, so an intercept
\eqn{c} in the same equation spans the same direction. The two are exactly
confounded: shifting \eqn{c} by \eqn{k} and \eqn{\omega} by
\eqn{-k(1 - \sum_j \xi_j)} leaves every predictor unchanged. A fitting layer
therefore estimates at most one of them. In \pkg{statmodels7} the intercept
is kept and \eqn{\omega} is held at zero, which a summary reports as
\code{omega (held)}; written without the intercept, as \code{0 + gas(...)}, the
level is estimated instead. The same holds for any set of columns whose
span contains the constant, such as a factor coded without an intercept.

The two spellings are one model with the same log-likelihood, loadings
and persistence, and their constants measure different quantities. The
score has zero conditional mean, so the level fluctuates around the fixed
point of the recursion,
\deqn{\mathrm{E}[f_t] = \frac{\omega}{1 - \sum_{j=1}^{q} \xi_j},}
and the intercept estimates this quantity when \eqn{\omega} is held.
Therefore
\deqn{\eta_t = c + f_t \;\text{with}\; \omega = 0
  \qquad\text{and}\qquad
  \eta_t = f_t \;\text{with}\; c = \frac{\omega}{1 - \sum_j \xi_j}}
describe the same predictor. With a persistence close to one the factor
\eqn{1/(1 - \sum_j \xi_j)} is large, so \eqn{\omega} is small, and its
standard error is not comparable with the intercept's: a change in
\eqn{\omega} moves the level by that factor. The identity is for a scalar
level; a level developed by a subformula is confounded with the part of
its span that the equation's design shares.
}

\subsection{Groups and time}{
\code{by} filters each group independently, as a panel of short series needs,
and \code{time} gives the order within a group. Only the order of the times
matters: a gap between two times counts as one step of the recursion.
Without \code{time} the rows are taken in the order in which they appear.
}

\subsection{The start of the recursion}{
With \code{init = "level"}, the default, the levels before the first
observation of a group equal the fixed point of the recursion,
\eqn{f_0 = \omega / (1 - \sum_j \xi_j)}, and so does the first level, so
the first predictor is the static predictor plus \eqn{f_0}. With
\code{init = "static"} the first predictor of every group equals a constant
\eqn{c} of that group that does not depend on the parameters: the first
level is \eqn{f_1 = c - \eta^{s}_1}, where \eqn{\eta^{s}_1} is the static
predictor at the group's first row, and the levels before it equal
\eqn{f_1}. The constants are the property \code{init_value}, one for every group
or one for all of them, which a fitting layer sets; \pkg{statmodels7} sets
them to the predictor of the model without the term at the first row of
each group, and keeps that model in the property \code{init_source}. With a
persistence close to one the first convention
makes the constant of the equation the starting value of the filter as
well as its level, and the likelihood can then have a second maximum at
which the constant fits the first observations; the second convention
separates the two. \code{init = "static"} is not yet available for a joint
filter whose terms carry subformulas.
}

\subsection{The starting points}{
A fitting layer fits the model from each of \code{n_start} starting points of
the term's own parameters and keeps the best (\code{\link[=term_starts]{term_starts()}}). Every
start has the level at zero and each score loading at \eqn{0.1}; they
differ in the first partial autocorrelation of the persistence, which is
\eqn{0} in the first start and \eqn{0.9}, \eqn{0.5}, \eqn{0.97} and
\eqn{0.7} in the following ones, the other partial autocorrelations
staying at zero. The likelihood of a filter can have a local maximum at a
low persistence beside the one at a high persistence, and a start without
persistence reaches the first; the start at \eqn{0.9} reaches the second.
\code{n_start = 1} fits from the first start only.
}

\subsection{A parameter developed with covariates}{
A two-sided formula in \code{...} whose left side names a parameter
develops it as \eqn{\psi_{j,t} = g_j^{-1}(z_t^\top\gamma_j)}, where the
design \eqn{Z} is built from the right-hand side through
\code{\link[=interpret_formula]{interpret_formula()}}, so the design takes the additive terms of
the package whose block does not move with their coefficients:
\preformatted{gas(p = 1, q = 1, omega ~ ridge(~g), kappa1 ~ s(x),
    pacf1 ~ random(~1 | id), by = id)}
The development acts on the unconstrained scale of the parameter's own
link, and therefore every per-observation value stays inside the
parameter's own set whatever the coefficients are: a loading on the
log link is positive at every observation, a persistence on the rhobit
chart is inside \eqn{(-1, 1)} at every observation, and at \eqn{q = 1}
that bounds the recursion's growth step by step. The coefficients
\eqn{\gamma_j} are the term's parameters, unconstrained and on the
identity link; the penalties that the sub-terms carry are reported through
\code{\link[=term_penalties]{term_penalties()}} under the key \code{parameter::subterm}.
A parameter that varies by observation changes the recursion itself,
\deqn{f_t = \omega_t + \sum_i a_{i,t}\, s_{t-i}
  + \sum_j b_{j,t}\, f_{t-j},}
with \eqn{b_t} from the Levinson-Durbin map of that observation's
partial autocorrelations, and the filter, its derivative, the reverse
pass and the curvature all run the general recursion.

\code{by = ~f} (a formula, where a grouping variable is a bare symbol)
is the shorthand giving the same subformula to every parameter; mixing
it with per-parameter formulas is an error. A structural term, and a
term whose block moves with its own coefficients (\code{\link[=seg]{seg()}}, \code{\link[=jump]{jump()}},
\code{\link[=jseg]{jseg()}} and \code{\link[=nl]{nl()}}), are rejected inside a subformula.
}

\subsection{A population value and a departure per group}{
The panel case is one subformula:
\code{gas(omega ~ random(~1 | id), by = id)} is a population value (the
intercept of the development) plus one unconstrained departure per
group, shrunk by the random intercept's own ridge, whose hyperparameter
a fitting layer estimates. \code{lasso(...)} in the subformula sets the
departures of the groups that do not need one exactly to zero.

The departures act on the unconstrained scale of the parameter's chart,
so a persistence stays inside \eqn{(-1, 1)} whatever the departure is.
The penalty on the departures also identifies them: a population value and
\eqn{m} departures are \eqn{m+1} numbers describing \eqn{m} group
values, so without the penalty the likelihood is flat along one
direction per developed parameter. This is the parametrization of a
random effect and it is identified the same way, there by a variance
component, here by the penalty. An unpenalized development over group
indicators is therefore for reading a filter at given parameters rather
than for fitting one.

The arguments \code{deviations} and \code{penalty} are not accepted and are
rejected by name; the subformula takes their place.
}
}
\examples{
# The term has a level, one loading and one persistence coordinate.
term_params(gas(p = 1, q = 1))
term_params(gas(p = 2, q = 2))

# The loading rides a log chart so that it stays positive, and the
# persistence a rhobit so that the filter stays stationary.
vapply(term_links(gas(p = 1, q = 2)), function(l) l@link_name,
       character(1))

# Running the filter: the term supplies the recursion and the caller
# supplies the density's score and curvature. Here the predictor is a
# Gaussian mean.
set.seed(1)
dd <- data.frame(t = 1:60, y = c(rnorm(30), rnorm(30, 3)))
b <- term_build(gas(p = 1, q = 1, time = t), dd)
out <- term_filter(b, eta = rep(0, 60), y = dd$y,
                   score = function(e, i) dd$y[i] - e,
                   curvature = function(e, i) -1,
                   psi = list(omega = 0.1, kappa1 = 0.3, pacf1 = 0.8))

# The level tracks the change in the mean.
round(out$eta[c(1, 15, 30, 32, 45, 60)], 3)

# The Jacobian that the filter propagates is exact; a central difference
# of the filter agrees with it to the accuracy of the step.
f <- function(v) sum(term_filter(b, rep(0, 60), dd$y,
                                 function(e, i) dd$y[i] - e,
                                 function(e, i) -1,
                                 list(omega = v[1], kappa1 = v[2],
                                      pacf1 = v[3]))$eta)
v0 <- c(0.1, 0.3, 0.8)
h  <- 1e-5
fd <- vapply(1:3, function(j) {
  e <- numeric(3); e[j] <- h
  (f(v0 + e) - f(v0 - e)) / (2 * h)
}, numeric(1))
max(abs(colSums(out$jacobian) - fd))

# A panel: the filter runs on each group from its own starting level.
dd$id <- rep(1:3, each = 20)
term_build(gas(p = 1, q = 1, by = id, time = t), dd)

# A developed parameter expands in place, and brings its sub-term's
# penalty with it.
dd$g <- factor(rep(c("u", "v"), 30))
gb <- term_build(gas(p = 1, q = 1, omega ~ ridge(~ g), time = t), dd)
term_params(gb)
vapply(term_penalties(gb), function(e) e$name, character(1))


# The term is fitted. The data are simulated from a known truth, so the
# estimates below can be read against it.
if (requireNamespace("statmodels7", quietly = TRUE)) {
  # The series is drawn from the recursion that the term implements,
  # with a gaussian mean.
  set.seed(2)
  om <- 0.05; al <- 0.3; be <- 0.9
  lev <- om / (1 - be)
  yy <- numeric(600)
  for (i in seq_len(600)) {
    yy[i] <- lev + rnorm(1)
    lev <- om + al * (yy[i] - lev) + be * lev
  }
  fd <- data.frame(t = seq_len(600), y = yy)
  ft <- statmodels7::statmod(y ~ 0 + gas(p = 1, q = 1, time = t),
                             distributions7::gaussian1_distrib(), fd)
  # The truth is omega 0.05, kappa 0.3, xi 0.9. xi is reported as the
  # autoregressive coefficient, whereas the estimated coordinate is a
  # partial autocorrelation.
  print(round(coef(ft)$mu, 3))

  # With an intercept the level is held at zero and the intercept
  # estimates omega / (1 - xi) instead: the same model, reparametrized.
  fi <- statmodels7::statmod(y ~ 1 + gas(p = 1, q = 1, time = t),
                             distributions7::gaussian1_distrib(), fd)
  print(c(logLik(ft), logLik(fi)))
  cf <- coef(ft)$mu
  c(intercept  = coef(fi)$mu[["(Intercept)"]],
    from_omega = cf[["gas.omega"]] / (1 - cf[["gas.xi1"]]))
}
}
\references{
Creal, D., Koopman, S. J. and Lucas, A. (2013). Generalized
autoregressive score models with applications. \emph{Journal of Applied
Econometrics}, 28(5), 777--795.

Harvey, A. C. (2013). \emph{Dynamic Models for Volatility and Heavy
Tails}. Cambridge University Press.
}
